<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">1693 Sicily earthquake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1693">1693</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Val di Noto earthquake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1693 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> earthquake struck south-eastern Sicily on 11 January 1693, after a foreshock two days earlier, and is the most destructive in Italian history. It killed about 60,000 people and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a>, Noto, Ragusa and dozens of other towns of the Val di Noto. Their reconstruction in a unified late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style produced the towns now inscribed as a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1693-sicily-earthquake.jpg" alt="1693 Sicily earthquake" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beric Dondarrion · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mappa_Terremoto_Val_di_Noto_1693.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
